/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Example_FiltersFIR FIR
 * @ingroup DspCore_Examples_Filters
 * @brief Compare 10- and 100-tap moving-average low-pass filters.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_FiltersFIR_Goal Goal
 * Process the same 300 Hz + 6 kHz signal with two runtime-sized FIR filters.
 * The longer average suppresses more high-frequency content, while its
 * linear-phase delay increases from 0.094 ms to 1.031 ms at 48 kHz.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_FiltersFIR_Run Run and render
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./build/macos_arm64/bin/Debug/DspCore_Filters_FIR_d --output ./output
 * ./build/macos_arm64/bin/Debug/render_signal.sh ./output/fir_moving_average.csv
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_FiltersFIR_Output Output
 * @code{.text}
 * DspCore :: FIR moving-average low-pass
 * Wrote signal data: ./output/fir_moving_average.csv
 * PNG: .../render_signal.sh ./output/fir_moving_average.csv
 * @endcode
 *
 * The top panel exposes the delay and smoothing in time. The middle panel
 * isolates the 300 Hz and 6 kHz components in a normalized DFT; the bottom
 * panel draws the coefficient-derived magnitude response, including the
 * deeper nulls of the 100-tap filter.
 *
 * \image html fir_moving_average.png "FIR moving-average tutorial output" width=900px
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_FiltersFIR_Code Desktop data generator
 * \include Filters/FIR/Desktop/main.cpp
 *
 * @subsection DspCore_Example_FiltersFIR_Kernel Portable FIR API use
 * \include Filters/FIR/Shared.cpp
 */
